Appendix C: Optimal Adaptive Bandwidth

Adaptive bandwidth AB \in [1, AB_{\max}] is the number of distinct, executable tactical states a team can occupy with a given squad and training time. We postulate an inverted‑U relationship with adaptability A:

A = g(AB) = a \cdot (AB)^p \cdot e^{-q \cdot AB}

with a, p, q > 0 and p < 1 to capture initial increasing returns to bandwidth and eventual dominance of coordination cost. The maximum occurs at:

\frac{dA}{dAB} = a \cdot e^{-q \cdot AB} \left( p \cdot AB^{\,p-1} - q \cdot AB^{\,p} \right) = 0

\Rightarrow p \cdot AB^{\,p-1} = q \cdot AB^{\,p} \Rightarrow AB^* = \frac{p}{q}

Thus AB^* is the optimal bandwidth. Below AB^*, the team is rigid; above, coordination costs (modelled implicitly through the exponential decay term) outweigh the benefits of additional options. Substituting AB^* back yields the maximal achievable adaptability A_{\max} = a \left(\frac{p}{q}\right)^p e^{-p}. Any team with AB far below or above AB^* will underperform relative to its talent.

Empirical calibration suggests plausible ranges: for elite international teams, p \approx 0.5, q \approx 0.1–0.2 gives AB^* in the 3–6 range, consistent with the tactical diversity observed in recent champions.

