Appendix G: Environmental Volatility Index for World Cup 2026

To quantify EV for the 2026 tournament, we construct a composite index based on:

1. Climate variability across venues: number of Köppen climate classes among host cities (≥3: humid subtropical, hot semi‑arid, Mediterranean, highland subtropical).
2. Altitude range: Mexico City (2,240 m) vs. sea‑level venues, requiring acclimation for non‑habituated teams.
3. Travel distance: average inter‑match distance for a typical group path, estimated at 3,500–5,500 km, among the highest in tournament history.
4. Match‑time heat stress: probability of WBGT > 28°C in afternoon fixtures (estimated at >30% for certain venues).

Normalised to a 0–1 scale, EV_{2026} \approx 0.83, compared to EV_{2018} \approx 0.45 (Russia, single country, moderate climate variation) and EV_{2022} \approx 0.60 (Qatar, compact but extreme heat). The high EV in 2026 implies that \lambda(EV) in the model is substantially greater than in previous editions, magnifying the effect of adaptation and adaptive capital.

